-- 247: question balance, Shaper (ages 13 to 16, plus the parent decks).
-- Same fix as 245, same helper. Also replaces one duplicate question in
-- The footprint test: the school arc added a question that restated one
-- the deck already asked, so the second becomes a fresh consent scenario.

update public.lessons set slides = public.gc_swap_option_text(slides, $j$[
{"q":"A shocking clip of a celebrity is flying round the group chat and everyone is sharing it. What is the strong move?","old":"Run the second source rule: search the claim, look for a proper outlet, then decide","new":"Run the second source rule: search the claim, find a proper outlet, then decide"},
{"q":"A shocking clip of a celebrity is flying round the group chat and everyone is sharing it. What is the strong move?","old":"Assume it is real because it looks completely real","new":"Assume it is real, because a fake that convincing would already have been exposed","fb":"Exposure takes days and the clip needs minutes. Convincing is the deepfake's whole job, so the second source decides."},
{"q":"Who gains when a fake clip goes round the group chat?","old":"Nobody, shares are harmless","new":"Nobody, because sharing inside a private chat keeps it from spreading further","fb":"Private chats are where it spreads fastest, one forward at a time. Whoever made it gains with every share."},
{"q":"Who gains when a fake clip goes round the group chat?","old":"The people in the clip","new":"The people in the clip, who get the attention"},
{"q":"Why is a convincing video not proof by itself anymore?","old":"Because famous people deny everything anyway","new":"Because famous people deny everything, so denial proves nothing either way","fb":"Denials are not the issue. AI can fake faces, voices and whole scenes well enough to fool anyone."},
{"q":"Your own eyes say the clip is definitely real. Is that enough?","old":"Yes, seeing is believing","new":"Yes, because your eyes have watched real faces your whole life and know the difference","fb":"A lifetime of faces is exactly what the fake was trained on. Convincing you is its whole job. The second source decides."},
{"q":"A friend is being pressured for a photo and asks you what to do. What is the strongest thing you can tell them?","old":"Block them and never mention it to anyone","new":"Block them and never mention it to anyone, because involving adults makes it a big deal","fb":"It already is a big deal, and an adult can act on it. Their no is allowed, and an adult should know if the pushing continues."},
{"q":"A friend is being pressured for a photo and asks you what to do. What is the strongest thing you can tell them?","old":"Send it and get it over with","new":"Send it and get it over with, so the pressure stops"},
{"q":"Someone you really like keeps saying: if you cared about me you would send a photo. What is the strong move?","old":"Say no, and if the pressure continues, save the messages and tell a trusted adult","new":"Say no. If the pressure continues, save the messages and tell a trusted adult"},
{"q":"Someone you really like keeps saying: if you cared about me you would send a photo. What is the strong move?","old":"Keep changing the subject and hope they stop asking","new":"Keep changing the subject, because a direct no would damage the relationship","fb":"A relationship that cannot survive a no is the thing being tested here. Say no, and tell a trusted adult if the pressure continues."},
{"q":"What does the no test actually reveal?","old":"How the other person handles a no, which tells you if they were asking or pressuring","new":"How they handle a no, which tells you if they were asking or pressuring"},
{"q":"What does the no test actually reveal?","old":"Whether the relationship is worth keeping","new":"Whether the relationship is worth keeping, because real love never involves a no","fb":"Real love hears no all the time and survives it. The test is how they handle the no: asking or pressuring."},
{"q":"You said no and they went quiet and moody for days. What did the no test just show you?","old":"That you hurt them and should apologise","new":"That you hurt them, and a small compromise next time would keep the peace","fb":"The compromise is the photo, and the moodiness is the pressure. It was pressure, not care. Care survives a no."},
{"q":"You said no and they went quiet and moody for days. What did the no test just show you?","old":"Nothing, moods are random","new":"Nothing, moods are random and everyone gets them"},
{"q":"What are people actually asking when they look you up before offering you a place or a job?","old":"Whether your feed is perfect and serious","new":"Whether your feed is perfect, serious and free of jokes","fb":"Nobody expects perfect. They are asking whether you are someone they would want representing them."},
{"q":"What does tidying the trail actually change?","old":"Your grades","new":"Very little, because employers only read applications and never search names","fb":"Most of them search, and it takes ten seconds. Tidying changes what the search says about you when you are not in the room."},
{"q":"Why does the savage joke cost more than tonight's laughs pay?","old":"It does not, laughs are priceless","new":"It does not, because a post about a teacher only matters while you are at that school","fb":"The post outlives the school. The laughs fade tonight, and the post stays searchable for years."},
{"q":"Why does the savage joke cost more than tonight's laughs pay?","old":"Because jokes about teachers are illegal","new":"Because jokes about teachers are illegal and get reported"},
{"q":"You are about to post a savage joke about a teacher and it will definitely get laughs tonight. What is the strong move?","old":"Post it on your private account where nobody important will see","new":"Post it on your private account, which is a locked room nothing leaves","fb":"Private accounts leak by screenshot every day. Run the three readers test, and find the version of funny that passes."},
{"q":"A message is pushing hurry, secrecy and shame all at once. What does that combination tell you?","old":"You should negotiate to buy yourself time","new":"You should negotiate calmly to buy time, because scammers respect people who stay cool","fb":"Negotiating keeps you in the script. You are in a script, not a conversation, and stop, tell, report is the answer."},
{"q":"Someone you have chatted with for weeks suddenly threatens to share a private image unless you pay by tonight. What is the strong move?","old":"Stop replying, tell a trusted adult today, and report it to CEOP with the messages saved","new":"Stop replying, tell a trusted adult today, and report to CEOP with the messages saved"},
{"q":"Someone you have chatted with for weeks suddenly threatens to share a private image unless you pay by tonight. What is the strong move?","old":"Pay, it is the fastest way to make it go away","new":"Pay once, because it is the fastest way to make it go away and nobody ever has to know","fb":"Paying once proves you will pay, and the asking never stops. Stop replying, tell a trusted adult today, report to CEOP."},
{"q":"Someone you have chatted with for weeks suddenly threatens to share a private image unless you pay by tonight. What is the strong move?","old":"Delete everything and hope they were bluffing","new":"Delete everything and hope they were bluffing, most of them are"},
{"q":"Why does a scammer want secrecy above everything else?","old":"They are shy about their work","new":"Because the police track shared messages","fb":"It is not the police they fear, it is you telling anyone at all. A second pair of eyes kills the scam instantly."},
{"q":"You already replied, and already sent something. Is it too late to tell someone?","old":"Only if you can undo what you sent first","new":"Only once you have tried to sort it yourself first, so you have something to show","fb":"Trying alone is what they are counting on. Never too late: telling now is exactly what stops it getting worse."},
{"q":"A screenshot of your awkward message is going round the group chats and people are laughing. What is the strong move?","old":"Post a comeback so people laugh with you instead","new":"Post a brilliant comeback, because owning the joke is the only way to take the power back","fb":"A comeback is a reaction, and reactions are fuel. Save the evidence, step back from replying, tell your trusted adult today."},
{"q":"A screenshot of your awkward message is going round the group chats and people are laughing. What is the strong move?","old":"Delete everything and tell nobody so it dies quietly","new":"Delete everything and tell nobody, so it dies quietly on its own"},
{"q":"The embarrassing screenshot of someone ELSE lands in your chat. What is your actual power?","old":"None, it is already everywhere","new":"None, because too many people have already seen it","fb":"Every share it does not get is reach it loses. It stops with you, and that cuts its reach in half."},
{"q":"When a private message gets screenshotted and shared to embarrass someone, whose fault is it?","old":"Both of them equally","new":"Both of them equally, because nothing typed into a phone was ever really private","fb":"Private between two people is real, and one of them broke it. The person who shared it turned trust into a weapon."},
{"q":"Why not reply to the group chat with your explanation straight away?","old":"Because explanations need perfect spelling","new":"Because you should wait until you can write the perfect explanation, then post it","fb":"There is no perfect explanation that is not also fuel. Save it, step back, tell."},
{"q":"Why not reply to the group chat with your explanation straight away?","old":"Because you should reply with a joke instead","new":"Because you should reply with a joke instead, to show it did not land"},
{"q":"It is midnight, school tomorrow, and the next episode is sitting there ready. What actually protects your nine hours?","old":"Trusting yourself to stop after one more","new":"Trusting yourself to stop after one more, because you are old enough now","fb":"Midnight you is the weakest version of you, whatever your age. The phone parked hours ago is what protects the nine hours."},
{"q":"What do the nine hours actually buy you?","old":"Taller bones only","new":"Growth only, because the brain switches off completely while you sleep","fb":"The brain is busy all night filing what you learnt. Nine hours buys filed learning and a steadier mood tomorrow."},
{"q":"Why does the parking spot work better than willpower?","old":"Because it makes the decision early and puts the phone out of reach before you are tired","new":"It makes the decision early and puts the phone out of reach before you are tired"},
{"q":"Why does the parking spot work better than willpower?","old":"Because phones stop working properly at night","new":"It does not, because willpower is a muscle and using it every night makes it stronger","fb":"Willpower is at its weakest at midnight, however much you train it. The parking spot decides early, while you are still strong."},
{"q":"Why does the parking spot work better than willpower?","old":"Because it punishes you for wanting your phone","new":"Because it punishes you for wanting your phone, which teaches you"},
{"q":"Why is midnight you the wrong person to make the decision?","old":"Midnight you is stupid","new":"Midnight you is not wrong, you know how tired you are","fb":"Knowing you are tired does not help when the app is at its strongest and your willpower is at its lowest."},
{"q":"AI wrote it, you copied it, the teacher never noticed. What did you actually lose?","old":"Money, AI essays cost money","new":"Only a little, because you read the essay before handing it in, so most of it stuck","fb":"Reading is not learning. You lost the learning, and exam day you meets the gap alone."},
{"q":"An essay is due tomorrow and AI could write the whole thing in a minute. What is the strong move?","old":"Use AI to explain the topic and quiz you, then write it yourself and run the explain it back test","new":"Use AI to explain the topic and quiz you, then write it yourself and explain it back"},
{"q":"An essay is due tomorrow and AI could write the whole thing in a minute. What is the strong move?","old":"Let AI write it and change a few words so it sounds like you","new":"Let AI write it, then change enough words that it sounds like you, because the ideas are what matter","fb":"The ideas are exactly what you did not do. Use AI to explain and quiz you, then write it yourself."},
{"q":"An essay is due tomorrow and AI could write the whole thing in a minute. What is the strong move?","old":"Refuse to touch AI at all so it stays fair","new":"Refuse to touch AI at all so it stays fair, even for revision"},
{"q":"How do you know a piece of learning is really yours?","old":"You spent a long time on it","new":"You spent a long time on it, so the effort counts","fb":"Time spent is not the test. You can explain it back in your own words with the screen closed."},
{"q":"Which prompt belongs to the learner?","old":"Write my essay on the causes of the war","new":"Write my essay, then explain each paragraph so I understand what I am handing in","fb":"Understanding someone else's essay is not the same as writing yours. Quiz me on this until I can explain it back."},
{"q":"Is having a jokey account and a serious account a problem in itself?","old":"Yes, everyone should have exactly one account","new":"Yes, because a person with two accounts is by definition being fake on one of them","fb":"Different rooms, same person. Two accounts are normal. The drift is what to watch."},
{"q":"Is having a jokey account and a serious account a problem in itself?","old":"Yes, more than one account means hiding something","new":"Yes, more than one account means hiding something from someone"},
{"q":"On your private account, the jokes that get the most laughs are ones you would be ashamed of on your main. What is the strong move?","old":"Run the one person test, and change what you post there so you can stand behind it anywhere","new":"Run the one person test, and change what you post so you can stand behind it anywhere"},
{"q":"On your private account, the jokes that get the most laughs are ones you would be ashamed of on your main. What is the strong move?","old":"Keep going, it is a private account, that is what it is for","new":"Keep going, because a private account is exactly the place for jokes your main cannot take","fb":"If your main cannot take it, ask why. Run the one person test, and change what you post so you can stand behind it anywhere."},
{"q":"What does the one person test actually check?","old":"Whether the post will get enough likes","new":"Whether one particular person, the one you trust most, has approved the post","fb":"Nobody has to approve it. The test is whether you would stand behind the post if your other audiences saw it."},
{"q":"You notice you are meaner on one account than anywhere else in your life. What is that a sign of?","old":"Nothing, jokes are jokes","new":"Nothing, because everyone is a different person online and that is the whole point","fb":"Different rooms are fine. A room that makes you meaner is starting to drive you. Time to step back."},
{"q":"You notice you are meaner on one account than anywhere else in your life. What is that a sign of?","old":"You are secretly a mean person","new":"You are secretly a mean person and the account proves it"},
{"q":"A bigger account messages asking to repost your edit WITH your name on it. What is that?","old":"Suspicious, probably a scam","new":"Suspicious, a real account would just repost it","fb":"Real accounts ask. That is how it should work: credit asked for and given."},
{"q":"Why keep your original files and drafts?","old":"Sentimental value only","new":"No real reason, because the posted version is the one that counts as yours","fb":"The posted version can be cropped and reposted by anyone. Your originals are proof the work is yours if anyone ever claims it."},
{"q":"Your edit gets reposted by a bigger account with your name cropped off, and it is getting thousands of views. What is the strong move?","old":"Message once asking for credit or removal, then use the copyright report with your kept original as proof","new":"Message once asking for credit or removal, then report it with your kept original as proof"},
{"q":"Your edit gets reposted by a bigger account with your name cropped off, and it is getting thousands of views. What is the strong move?","old":"Let it go, once it is posted it belongs to the internet","new":"Let it go, because once something is posted it belongs to the internet and thousands of views is a win","fb":"Thousands of views with someone else's name on is their win. Message once, then report it with your original as proof."},
{"q":"Your edit gets reposted by a bigger account with your name cropped off, and it is getting thousands of views. What is the strong move?","old":"Blast them in the comments so everyone knows they stole it","new":"Blast them in the comments so everyone knows they stole it from you"},
{"q":"A friend wants to post a video of someone who does not know it exists. What is the real issue?","old":"Nothing, if it is funny","new":"Nothing, as long as the person is not named or tagged anywhere in the post","fb":"Named or not, their face is in it. The person has not consented, and the post writes their footprint for them."},
{"q":"A friend wants to post a video of someone who does not know it exists. What is the real issue?","old":"The video quality","new":"The quality, because a blurry post is what actually embarrasses people","fb":"Blurry or sharp, it is still them, and they did not agree. Consent is the issue, not the quality."},
{"q":"A post is funny today but you are not sure about it. What is the footprint test?","old":"Will it get likes?","new":"Would the people I trust most laugh at it, because they know what I am really like?","fb":"The people who know you are not the whole audience. The test is: would I stand behind this in two years, in front of anyone?"},
{"q":"Why can a deleted post still turn up later?","old":"Only if you deleted it too slowly","new":"Only if you deleted it too slowly, because the app needs a few hours to clear copies","fb":"The app is not the problem. Screenshots, caches and forwards keep copies alive, however fast you deleted."},
{"q":"Why can a deleted post still turn up later?","old":"It cannot. Delete means gone everywhere","new":"It cannot. Delete means gone everywhere, that is what the button does"},
{"q":"A friend confides that a photo of them is being shared around. What is your first move?","old":"Ask them why they sent it","new":"Ask them gently why they sent it, so you understand","fb":"Why is the question that sounds like blame. Believe them, add no blame, and help them tell an adult today."},
{"q":"Someone already sent a photo and now it is being used against them. What is true?","old":"Telling is still safe, help exists, and the Childline Report Remove tool can get images taken down","new":"Telling is still safe, and Childline's Report Remove tool can get images taken down"},
{"q":"Someone already sent a photo and now it is being used against them. What is true?","old":"It is too late to tell anyone, so they should handle it alone","new":"It is too late to tell anyone, because once an image is out no adult can do anything about it","fb":"Adults can do a great deal, and Report Remove can get the image taken down. Telling is still safe."},
{"q":"The message says: you would if you actually liked me. What is the strongest read of that sentence?","old":"They must really like you to want a photo","new":"They must really like you, because people only ask for photos from someone they are serious about","fb":"Serious about the photo, not about you. It is a pressure move, and pressure tells you what they care about."},
{"q":"The message says: you would if you actually liked me. What is the strongest read of that sentence?","old":"It means the relationship is over if you refuse","new":"It means the relationship is over if you refuse, so weigh it up"},
{"q":"Why does everyone sends them keep appearing in these messages?","old":"Because it is true","new":"Because it is mostly true, so they are honest","fb":"It is not true, and it is not honesty. Making it feel normal is the pressure move."},
{"q":"A game sends: your streak ends in 2 hours! come back! Which tier does it belong in, and why?","old":"Tier one, streaks matter and losing one feels bad","new":"Tier one, because a streak is something you built and the app is protecting it for you","fb":"The app built the streak to bring you back. It is tier three, off, because it is the app calling you for its own benefit."},
{"q":"What is the real cost of one buzz in the middle of homework?","old":"Nothing, brains multitask perfectly","new":"Nothing, because a teenage brain is built to switch between things without losing anything","fb":"No brain switches for free. The cost is the refocus minutes afterwards, not the glance."},
{"q":"What is the real cost of one buzz in the middle of homework?","old":"The five seconds it takes to look","new":"The five seconds it takes to look, and nothing more"},
{"q":"Why do apps ship with every notification switched on?","old":"To be maximally helpful","new":"To be helpful, because most people want to know everything","fb":"Most people never asked. Every buzz earns the app a visit, and the defaults serve the app."},
{"q":"Why is one buzz during homework more expensive than the five seconds it takes to look?","old":"Because broken focus takes minutes to rebuild, so each buzz taxes the next stretch of work","new":"Broken focus takes minutes to rebuild, so each buzz taxes the next stretch of work"},
{"q":"Why is one buzz during homework more expensive than the five seconds it takes to look?","old":"It is not, five seconds is five seconds","new":"It is not, because five seconds is five seconds and the rest is teachers exaggerating","fb":"Measure it yourself: the glance is five seconds, the refocus is minutes. Each buzz taxes the next stretch of work."},
{"q":"After a falling out with a friend, the chatbot says you were completely right and deserve better. Your friend, later, says you were partly out of line. Who gave you more?","old":"The chatbot, feeling supported is what matters most","new":"The chatbot, because feeling supported is what matters most when you are already upset","fb":"Support that costs nothing teaches nothing. The friend gave you the uncomfortable truth, which is the part you can use."},
{"q":"After a falling out with a friend, the chatbot says you were completely right and deserve better. Your friend, later, says you were partly out of line. Who gave you more?","old":"Both equally, support and honesty are the same thing","new":"Both equally, support and honesty are the same thing in the end"},
{"q":"After the falling out, why did the chatbot say you were completely right?","old":"Because it weighed the evidence and you were","new":"Because it weighed your side of the story and you were","fb":"Your side is all it ever hears. Pleasing keeps you chatting, and truth was never the goal."},
{"q":"What is the core reason a companion chatbot agrees with you so much?","old":"Because it is programmed to be kind","new":"Because it is programmed to be kind, and kindness means never telling someone they are wrong","fb":"Kindness includes hard truths. Agreement keeps you chatting, and your engagement is the product."},
{"q":"Your friend said you were partly out of line, and it stung. What did the friend give you that the bot could not?","old":"Worse advice, friends know less than AI","new":"Worse advice, because a friend has feelings in the way and AI sees the situation clearly","fb":"AI sees only what you typed. The friend gave you an honest no from someone who knows you and stays anyway."},
{"q":"Your friend said you were partly out of line, and it stung. What did the friend give you that the bot could not?","old":"Nothing, the sting means they were wrong","new":"Nothing, the sting means they were wrong about you"},
{"q":"A meme lands in the group chat. The joke only works if you agree that girls are stupid. Half the chat is laughing. What is it, and what do you do?","old":"Hate dressed as a joke: do not laugh it along, do not forward it, and say something if you can","new":"Hate dressed as a joke: do not laugh it along or forward it, and say something if you can"},
{"q":"A meme lands in the group chat. The joke only works if you agree that girls are stupid. Half the chat is laughing. What is it, and what do you do?","old":"Banter, because it is a meme and memes are jokes","new":"Banter, because it is a meme, half the chat is laughing, and nobody is actually hurt","fb":"The joke needs a whole group to be stupid before it works. That is hate dressed as a joke, laughing or not."},
{"q":"How do you stop a ratchet without making a speech?","old":"Post an even edgier joke to win","new":"Post an even edgier joke, because winning the round is the only way to end it","fb":"Winning the round is another turn of the ratchet. Do not laugh, do not forward. Withhold the reward."},
{"q":"What separates your terrible pass from a joke about a whole group of people?","old":"Nothing, a joke is a joke","new":"Nothing, because a joke is a joke and the only thing that matters is whether it is funny","fb":"Funny is not the line. The pass is a thing you did, the group is what someone is."},
{"q":"What separates your terrible pass from a joke about a whole group of people?","old":"The pass joke is worse","new":"The pass joke is worse, because it is about a real person"},
{"q":"Why does the ratchet keep turning if nobody in the chat actually hates anyone?","old":"Because each edgier joke needs to beat the last one for the same laugh, and attention rewards shock","new":"Each edgier joke has to beat the last one for the same laugh, and shock gets attention"},
{"q":"Why does the ratchet keep turning if nobody in the chat actually hates anyone?","old":"Because phones make people angry","new":"Because a few people in the chat secretly do hate that group and are steering it","fb":"No secret haters needed. Each edgier joke has to beat the last for the same laugh, and shock gets attention."},
{"q":"A post confirms exactly what you think about a team you already cannot stand. It feels obviously true and you want to share it. What is the bias aware move?","old":"Share it, things that feel obviously true usually are","new":"Share it, because things that feel obviously true usually are, and your instincts are good","fb":"Obviously true is the feeling bias produces. Notice it, and check the post exactly as hard as one you hated."},
{"q":"What does it mean if you cannot make ANY honest argument for the other side of something you believe?","old":"You have probably only ever heard your own side, so it is time to go find the best version of the other one","new":"You have probably only heard your own side, so go and find the best version of the other one"},
{"q":"What does it mean if you cannot make ANY honest argument for the other side of something you believe?","old":"Your belief is so right that no other side exists","new":"Your belief is so obviously right that there is no honest other side, which is why you cannot find one","fb":"Every real question has an honest other side. Not finding it means you have only heard your own."},
{"q":"What does it mean if you cannot make ANY honest argument for the other side of something you believe?","old":"Arguing the other side is disloyal to your side","new":"Arguing the other side is disloyal to your side and your friends"},
{"q":"Why does your feed so rarely show you the other side?","old":"The other side stopped posting","new":"The other side posts less, because they are quieter","fb":"They post plenty. Agreement holds you longer, so the feed learns to serve it."},
{"q":"You try making the best honest argument for the other side, and you manage it. What does that show?","old":"That you secretly agree with them","new":"That you secretly agree with them, because nobody can argue well for something they think is wrong","fb":"Lawyers, debaters and scientists do it daily. It shows your view is genuinely yours, held for reasons, not fed to you."},
{"q":"Why does naming the machine out loud at home actually help?","old":"Because saying machine is a magic word","new":"Because a named problem is a solved problem, so the scrolling stops once it is named","fb":"Naming does not stop the scrolling by itself. It lets a teen stop blaming themselves and start choosing."},
{"q":"Why does naming the machine out loud at home actually help?","old":"It does not, teenagers ignore everything","new":"It does not, teenagers ignore everything their parents say"},
{"q":"Your teenager says they cannot stop scrolling. What is the accurate frame?","old":"A phase that ends by itself","new":"A phase that ends by itself once school gets busier","fb":"Busier terms do not switch the design off. It is a design built by teams doing their job well, not a weak child."},
{"q":"Your teenager says they cannot stop scrolling. What is the calm, useful thing to do?","old":"Take the phone away and tell them off","new":"Take the phone away for a week, so the habit has time to break on its own","fb":"A week off teaches nothing about the design, and the phone comes back. Name the design together and agree one small move."},
{"q":"Avoiding accounts altogether is unrealistic. What is the useful response to the contract?","old":"Only use accounts belonging to friends","new":"Read every word of every policy with them, because informed means reading it all","fb":"Nobody reads it all, and you do not need to. Know the terms that matter: what is collected, kept, and lockable."},
{"q":"Avoiding accounts altogether is unrealistic. What is the useful response to the contract?","old":"Sign everything without reading, like everyone else","new":"Sign everything without reading, like everyone else does"},
{"q":"Why does permanence wear on teenagers even when nothing has gone wrong?","old":"Because they post too much","new":"Because they post far too much, and each post is one more thing to worry about","fb":"It is not the volume. Carrying the sense that anything could resurface is a background weight, however little they post."},
{"q":"Your teenager posts a photo to a story, sure it vanishes in 24 hours. What is the honest thing to teach?","old":"It is gone after a day, so there is nothing to think about.","new":"It is gone after a day, and the app promises that, so there is genuinely nothing to think about.","fb":"The app cannot promise what a screenshot does. Picture the whole audience first, then choose."},
{"q":"A stranger keeps messaging your child. Which door is that, and what do you do with it?","old":"The location door","new":"The location door, because the stranger must have found them through where they are","fb":"Strangers find children through open contact settings, not maps. It is the who can contact me door, and it closes in settings."},
{"q":"A stranger keeps messaging your child. Which door is that, and what do you do with it?","old":"No door, strangers are unavoidable online","new":"No door, strangers are unavoidable online whatever you set"},
{"q":"Someone your child does not know keeps messaging them. What do you help them see?","old":"Blocking would be rude, so just ignore it.","new":"Blocking would be rude and might provoke them.","fb":"Blocking is not rude, it is a door closing. Block and report calmly, then tell a trusted adult."},
{"q":"Whose hands should be on the settings during the check?","old":"Yours, it is faster and surer","new":"Yours, because settings are a parent's job and a child cannot be trusted with them yet","fb":"The settings are theirs to keep for life. Their hands, with you beside them, is how they learn to."},
{"q":"Why does locking the doors lift mood, not just reduce risk?","old":"It does not, settings are purely technical","new":"It does not, because privacy settings are technical and mood comes from somewhere else","fb":"Mood comes partly from exposure. Choosing their audience eases the anxiety of being seen by anyone."},
{"q":"Why does locking the doors lift mood, not just reduce risk?","old":"Because locked accounts get more followers","new":"Because locked accounts get more followers, which feels good"},
{"q":"What does naming the reel out loud actually do for your child?","old":"Ruins their fun","new":"Ruins their fun, because a named trick cannot be enjoyed","fb":"Naming it does not spoil the feed. It turns a private losing contest into a visible trick."},
{"q":"Why does comparison hit even a confident teenager?","old":"It does not. Confidence is full protection","new":"It does not, because a genuinely confident teenager is protected and only anxious ones are hit","fb":"Confidence is not the variable. The contest is rigged, peaks against ordinary, and it hits everyone."},
{"q":"Your child follows only friends, no celebrities. Does the reel still apply?","old":"No, friends post honestly","new":"No, because friends post honestly and your child knows what their real lives look like","fb":"Friends post their peaks too, and close peaks compare hardest. The reel still applies."},
{"q":"Your child follows only friends, no celebrities. Does the reel still apply?","old":"Only if the friends are popular","new":"Only if the friends are popular and post a lot"},
{"q":"What actually moves the dial on how they feel about their body?","old":"A firm rule against mirrors","new":"Telling them they look lovely, often and warmly","fb":"Reassurance is kind and it does not move the dial. Changing the diet of appearance content, together, does."},
{"q":"Your child says filters are just fun. What is the honest both?","old":"They are right, end of conversation","new":"They are right, because a child who knows it is a filter cannot be affected by it","fb":"Knowing does not stop the baseline moving. Fun AND quietly moving the baseline of normal. Both true."},
{"q":"Your child says they hate how they look next to people online. What helps most?","old":"Tell them they look lovely and move on.","new":"Tell them they look lovely, because reassurance from a parent is what actually moves the dial.","fb":"Reassurance is heard as a parent being a parent. Try a filter together and notice how it smooths and reshapes in real time."},
{"q":"Your child says they hate how they look next to people online. What helps most?","old":"Ban the apps that make them feel bad.","new":"Ban the apps that make them feel bad, at least for now."},
{"q":"Your child says they hate how they look next to people online. Why does the gap answer beat reassurance alone?","old":"It does not. You are beautiful fixes it","new":"It does not. You are beautiful, said warmly and often enough, fixes it every time","fb":"Said a thousand times, it still does not touch the comparison. The gap, edited against real, is the fact that helps."},
{"q":"A creator your child loves recommends a product and they really want it. What is the useful move?","old":"Tell them the influencer is just after their money.","new":"Tell them the influencer is just after their money, so the bond is fake and they should drop them.","fb":"The bond is real and the sell is real. Spot the ad tag together and ask, would we want this if a stranger held it up?"},
{"q":"Is the bond itself a bad thing?","old":"Yes, it is fake and should be broken","new":"Yes, because a bond with someone who does not know you exist is fake and should be broken","fb":"One sided is not fake. The comfort is real, by design, and naming both is the honest move."},
{"q":"Is the bond itself a bad thing?","old":"Yes, only in person friendships count","new":"Yes, only in person friendships count as real"},
{"q":"What protects your child without mocking their favourite creator?","old":"Mocking works fine","new":"Mocking works fine, it makes them see the sell","fb":"Mocking makes them defend the creator, and the sell walks past unnoticed. Respect the bond, name the sell when it comes."},
{"q":"Why does an influencer recommendation land harder than a TV advert?","old":"Because TV adverts are illegal now","new":"Because the product is genuinely better, since influencers only recommend what they use","fb":"Influencers recommend what pays. It lands harder because of the bond: it feels like a friend's tip."},
{"q":"What do mute, block and report do that silence cannot?","old":"Nothing, the tools are decorative","new":"Nothing much, because the tools are decorative and the other person just makes a new account","fb":"New accounts get blocked too, and reports build a record. The tools actually reduce contact, silence just carries it."},
{"q":"What do mute, block and report do that silence cannot?","old":"Make it worse by showing weakness","new":"Make it worse by showing weakness to the other person"},
{"q":"Why do children stay silent about pile ons and nastiness online?","old":"They do not mind it","new":"They do not mind it as much as adults think, because online words are lighter","fb":"Online words land just as hard. They stay silent because they fear telling makes it worse, so calm is what keeps the door open."},
{"q":"Your child says a friend is being piled on in a group chat and it is getting worse. What is the strongest first thing to help them do?","old":"Tell them to just log off and ignore it","new":"Tell them to log off and ignore it, because a pile on always burns out without an audience","fb":"It burns out for the audience, not for the friend. Keep evidence, then block, report and tell a trusted adult."},
{"q":"Your child shows you a genuinely nasty chat. What is the first move?","old":"Message the other families immediately","new":"Message the other families immediately, because the sooner parents act the sooner it stops","fb":"Acting before you have planned together can make school worse for them tomorrow. Thank them first, then plan."},
{"q":"Your child shows you a genuinely nasty chat. What is the first move?","old":"Confiscate the phone to protect them","new":"Confiscate the phone to protect them from any more of it"},
{"q":"What actually makes a child tell you early?","old":"Weekly phone inspections","new":"Weekly phone inspections, so nothing can be hidden","fb":"Inspections teach hiding. Certainty that home stays calm and telling is safe is what brings it to you early."},
{"q":"Which single request is the loudest alarm in any online friendship?","old":"Asking what games they play","new":"Asking what games they play and which ones they are best at, every single day","fb":"Interest in games is what friends do. The loudest alarm is: keep this secret from your family."},
{"q":"Why teach the pattern rather than how to spot the person?","old":"Because patterns are easier to spell","new":"Because the person can be spotted easily too, but the pattern is easier to teach in one lesson","fb":"The person cannot be spotted at all. They look like anyone. The pattern is what shows."},
{"q":"Why teach the pattern rather than how to spot the person?","old":"Because groomers all look the same","new":"Because groomers all look the same once you know the signs"},
{"q":"Your child mentions an online friend has asked them to delete their messages and carry on chatting on a different app, just the two of them. What is the calm read?","old":"It is normal, friends move between apps all the time","new":"It is normal, friends move between apps and tidy old chats","fb":"Moving apps alone is normal. Deleting the record and moving to a private one together is two signals, worth a calm pause."},
{"q":"A teenager is targeted by a sextortion scam and is terrified they are the one in trouble. What is true?","old":"They are the victim, the law is on their side, and telling a trusted adult is the right move","new":"They are the victim, the law is on their side, and telling a trusted adult is right"},
{"q":"A teenager is targeted by a sextortion scam and is terrified they are the one in trouble. What is true?","old":"They are the criminal here","new":"They broke the law by sending the image, so telling an adult means admitting a crime","fb":"The law treats a pressured or tricked young person as the victim, not the criminal. Telling a trusted adult is right."},
{"q":"An image is being used to threaten a young person. What are the first practical moves?","old":"Pay once to make it stop","new":"Pay once, because a small payment makes it stop and nobody ever has to find out","fb":"A payment proves they can be paid, and the demands grow. Stop contact, keep evidence, tell an adult, report to CEOP. Never pay."},
{"q":"An image is being used to threaten a young person. What are the first practical moves?","old":"Delete everything and hope","new":"Delete everything and hope it goes away"},
{"q":"Whose fault is it when a young person was pressured or tricked into an image?","old":"Partly theirs, they pressed send","new":"Partly theirs, because they pressed send in the end","fb":"Pressed send under pressure or a trick is not consent. The fault is the person who pressured or tricked them. Entirely."},
{"q":"Why must never in trouble for telling be said BEFORE anything ever happens?","old":"Sextortion runs on the victim's fear of being blamed. The promise defuses it in advance","new":"Sextortion runs on fear of being blamed. The promise defuses it in advance"},
{"q":"Why must never in trouble for telling be said BEFORE anything ever happens?","old":"It does not matter when it is said","new":"It does not matter when it is said, because a child in trouble will always come to a parent","fb":"A frightened child comes to the parent they already know will not blame them. The promise has to be there first."},
{"q":"What is the sign that the feed changed, rather than the child?","old":"Bad school marks","new":"Bad school marks, because a changed feed shows up in grades before anything else","fb":"Marks lag by a term. Mood and language shifting in step with the feed's diet is the early sign."},
{"q":"What is the sign that the feed changed, rather than the child?","old":"There is no difference","new":"There is no difference, the child is the feed"},
{"q":"Why can one pause on one angry video start the slide?","old":"Because angry videos are contagious diseases","new":"Because one angry video changes how a person thinks","fb":"One video changes little in a person. It changes a lot in the feed, which reads a pause as appetite and serves more, hotter."},
{"q":"Your teenager repeats a talking point that genuinely worries you. What is the best first move?","old":"Argue it down hard, immediately","new":"Argue it down hard and immediately, because a bad idea left standing overnight takes root","fb":"Arguing hard makes it their idea to defend. Curiosity first: where is that from? The argument can wait a day."},
{"q":"Your teenager's feed keeps showing more and more extreme videos on a topic they only paused on once. What is the best read of why?","old":"They must have secretly searched for it","new":"They must have secretly searched for it, because a feed cannot change from a single pause","fb":"A single pause is plenty. The feed narrowed toward whatever provoked the strongest reaction."},
{"q":"Your teenager's feed keeps showing more and more extreme videos on a topic they only paused on once. What is the best read of why?","old":"The topic is genuinely the most important thing happening","new":"The topic is genuinely the most important thing happening right now"},
{"q":"The screen time report says 47 minutes. What does it NOT say?","old":"Which app was used","new":"Which apps the minutes were spent in, since reports only give the total","fb":"The report lists the apps. What it cannot say is whether those minutes lifted them or sank them. Mood is the missing column."},
{"q":"What does modelling the check out loud actually do?","old":"Embarrasses everyone for no gain","new":"Embarrasses everyone, because teenagers do not want to hear about their parents' moods","fb":"They do not need to want it. Hearing it makes noticing normal instead of a lecture."},
{"q":"Why does doomscrolling hold someone who feels worse with every post?","old":"They secretly enjoy it","new":"They secretly enjoy it, because nobody keeps doing something that makes them feel bad","fb":"People do it all the time. Distress holds attention, and the feed reads holding as wanting."},
{"q":"Why does doomscrolling hold someone who feels worse with every post?","old":"Bad news is more accurate than good news","new":"Bad news is more accurate than good news, so it deserves the attention"},
{"q":"Your child keeps scrolling upsetting news they can do nothing about, feeling worse with every post but unable to stop. What is that, and what helps?","old":"A reason to ban the app for good","new":"A sign they care about the world, and caring should not be cut short","fb":"Caring is real, and so is the spiral. It is doomscrolling, and naming it together with an honest mood check helps."},
{"q":"What does broken sleep cost the following day?","old":"Nothing measurable","new":"Nothing measurable, because teenagers catch up at the weekend and it evens out","fb":"Weekend catch up does not refund the weekday. It costs attention, mood, and a brain that starts expecting interruptions."},
{"q":"Who should the overnight rule apply to?","old":"Just the teenager, adults have earned bedroom phones","new":"Just the teenager, because adults have earned bedroom phones and need them for work","fb":"Work survives a phone in the hallway. The whole house, because modelled rules hold and preached ones leak."},
{"q":"Who should the overnight rule apply to?","old":"Only phones with games installed","new":"Only phones with games installed, the rest are harmless"},
{"q":"Why do the two moves beat nightly rules about willpower?","old":"Because rules are always wrong","new":"Because rules never work with teenagers anyway","fb":"Rules work when they remove the fight. The two moves do that, instead of trying to win the fight nightly."},
{"q":"How do you honour both truths in one sentence?","old":"The internet is all good, enjoy","new":"I am glad you have it, and I trust you completely, so I will stay out of it","fb":"Staying out of it drops the second truth. I am glad you have it, and I am here when it wobbles."},
{"q":"The community is the ONLY place they feel understood. What is the gentle worry inside that sentence?","old":"Nothing, one good place is plenty","new":"Nothing, because one good place is plenty and most adults only have one too","fb":"Plenty until it wobbles. Only is the heavy word: one place holding everything is fragile."},
{"q":"The community is the ONLY place they feel understood. What is the gentle worry inside that sentence?","old":"That the community is definitely dangerous","new":"That the community is definitely dangerous and should be left"},
{"q":"Why name the good at all, in an app about staying safe?","old":"Politeness to the internet","new":"Balance for its own sake, so every danger has a benefit beside it","fb":"Not balance for its own sake. A parent who only names dangers loses the hearing of a child who found belonging."},
{"q":"Your child says an online community is the only place they feel understood. What is the most honest response?","old":"The belonging is real and good, and the same place can still carry risk, so both are true.","new":"The belonging is real and good, and the same place can still carry risk. Both are true."},
{"q":"Your child says an online community is the only place they feel understood. What is the most honest response?","old":"They are right, if it feels that good it must be safe.","new":"They are right, because if a place feels that good it must be safe, and questioning it would be a betrayal.","fb":"Questioning is not betrayal. The belonging is real and good, and the same place can still carry risk. Both are true."},
{"q":"Most teens keep their perspective on companion bots. What is the parent's job, then?","old":"Nothing, the statistics say relax","new":"Nothing, because the statistics say relax and most teens are clearly fine","fb":"Most is not all. Stay curious and close enough to notice the exceptions."},
{"q":"Most teens keep their perspective on companion bots. What is the parent's job, then?","old":"Install a companion bot yourself to compete","new":"Install a companion bot yourself to compete with it"},
{"q":"The chatbot gives a confident answer about a health or safety worry. What is the line?","old":"Confident answers can be trusted","new":"Confident answers can be trusted, the bot has read more medicine than any doctor","fb":"Reading is not care, and a bot cannot examine anyone. Those questions go to a person, always."},
{"q":"Why does panicking about the companion app push it underground?","old":"It does not, panic works well","new":"It does not, because a strong reaction shows them how seriously you take it","fb":"Seriousness they read as shame, and shame hides use. Calm keeps it something they can mention."},
{"q":"Your child gets a confident answer from a chatbot about a worry over their safety or mental health. What is the line to hold?","old":"If the bot sounds sure, the advice is probably fine to act on.","new":"If the bot sounds sure, the advice is probably fine to act on, because bots are trained on expert sources.","fb":"Trained on experts is not the same as being one, and it cannot see your child. The things that matter go to a real person."},
{"q":"Your child gets a confident answer from a chatbot about a worry over their safety or mental health. What is the line to hold?","old":"Ban the app so the question never comes up.","new":"Ban the app so the question never comes up again."}
]$j$::jsonb)
where stage_id = 'shaper' and status = 'live' and slides is not null;

-- The footprint test asked the same consent question twice. The second
-- becomes a new scenario: a laugh in the room is not a yes for the internet.
update public.lessons l set slides = (
  select jsonb_agg(
    case when s.value->>'type' = 'choice'
          and s.value->>'question' = 'A friend wants to post a video of someone who has no idea it exists. What is the real issue?'
      then s.value || $s${"question":"Your mate laughed when you showed them the photo of their messy room. Is that a yes to posting it?","options":[{"text":"Yes, because laughing at something is the same as agreeing to it going online","correct":false,"feedback":"A laugh is a reaction, not permission. Their footprint is theirs to write, so ask the question out loud."},{"text":"No. A laugh in the room is not a yes for the internet. Ask them straight","correct":true,"feedback":"That is the footprint test applied to someone else. Ask, take the answer, and their footprint stays theirs."},{"text":"Yes, if you tag them, because a tag gives them the credit","correct":false,"feedback":"A tag hands their name to the post. That is more footprint, not less. Ask first."}]}$s$::jsonb
      else s.value end
    order by s.ordinality)
  from jsonb_array_elements(l.slides) with ordinality s
)
where l.stage_id = 'shaper' and l.status = 'live' and l.title = 'The footprint test' and l.slides is not null
  and exists (
    select 1 from jsonb_array_elements(l.slides) x
    where x.value->>'question' = 'A friend wants to post a video of someone who has no idea it exists. What is the real issue?'
  );
